(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-breast-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Breast Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Between which ribs does the breast lie?","choices":["The 3rd and 8th rib","The 1st and 3rd rib","The 2nd and 6th rib","The 5th and 9th rib"],"answer":2,"exp":"The breast lies between the 2nd and 6th rib, spanning the sternal edge to the midaxillary line. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"What proportion of the breast lies superficial to the pectoral muscle versus the serratus anterior?","choices":["Entirely superficial to the serratus anterior only","Entirely superficial to the pectoral muscle only","1/3 superficial to the pectoral muscle, 2/3 superficial to the serratus anterior","2/3 superficial to the pectoral muscle, 1/3 superficial to the serratus anterior"],"answer":3,"exp":"The breast is 2/3 superficial to the pectoral muscle and 1/3 superficial to the serratus anterior. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the suspensory ligament of the breast called, and what does it connect?","choices":["Stensen's ligament, connecting glandular lobes","Wharton's ligament, connecting the nipple to the areola","Poupart's ligament, connecting the breast to the sternum","Cooper's ligament, connecting skin to breast fascia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cooper's ligament is the fibrous suspensory ligament connecting the skin to the breast fascia. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"How is the breast's glandular tissue organized?","choices":["In lobes around the nipple, with a duct draining each lobe","Ducts merge into a single duct before reaching the nipple","In lobes surrounding the axilla rather than the nipple region","In a single continuous sheet with no lobar organization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glandular tissue is organized into lobes around the nipple, each drained by its own duct. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"What supplies the lateral quadrants of the breast vascularly?","choices":["Pectoral branches of the thoracoacromial artery and the external mammary branch of the lateral thoracic artery","Branches of the anterior intercostal arteries alone, without any contribution from the thoracic vessels","The femoral artery, via deep branches that ascend along the anterior chest wall and axilla","Perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery, which cross the sternum medially"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lateral quadrants are supplied by pectoral branches of the thoracoacromial branch of the axillary artery and the external mammary branch of the lateral thoracic artery. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of breast lymphatic drainage empties into the axillary lymph nodes?","choices":["Approximately 25%","Nearly 100%","Approximately 75%","Approximately 50%"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 75% of breast lymphatic drainage empties into the axillary lymph nodes. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Where do the medial portions of the breast primarily drain lymphatically?","choices":["Inguinal lymph nodes in the groin region","Deep cervical lymph nodes in the neck","Internal mammary (parasternal) lymph nodes","Axillary lymph nodes exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial breast portions drain into the internal mammary (parasternal) lymph nodes. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which axillary node group drains most of the breast tissue, located at the lower border of pectoralis major inside the axillary fold?","choices":["Infraclavicular nodes","Pectoral (anterior) nodes","Central nodes","Subscapular (posterior) nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pectoral (anterior) nodes, at the lower border of pectoralis major inside the axillary fold, drain most of the breast tissue. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which axillary lymph node group is described as the most frequently palpable?","choices":["Lateral nodes","Infra- and supraclavicular nodes","Central nodes","Subscapular (posterior) nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Central nodes, located high in the axilla, are the most frequently palpable of the axillary node groups. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Besides the axilla and internal mammary nodes, where else might inferior breast lymphatics drain?","choices":["There are no alternative drainage pathways beyond the axillary basin","Only the inguinal lymph nodes situated in the groin, far from the breast's true drainage basin","Subdiaphragmatic nodes, abdominal lymphatics, and hepatic lymphatic pathways","Only the contralateral breast tissue, never structures below the diaphragm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inferior breast lymphatics may drain into subdiaphragmatic nodes, abdominal lymphatics, and hepatic lymphatic pathways. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are Montgomery tubercles?","choices":["Small, rounded elevations on the areolar surface marking sebaceous glands","The four anatomic quadrants used to describe breast tissue location","Lactiferous ducts that open directly at the tip of the nipple","The tail of Spence, a wedge of breast tissue extending into the axilla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Montgomery tubercles (glandulae areolares) are small rounded elevations on the areola marking sebaceous glands. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the tail of Spence?","choices":["The nipple's lactiferous duct opening","A pigmented area around the nipple","A breast segment that extends into the axilla","A sebaceous gland elevation on the areola"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tail of Spence is the portion of breast tissue that extends into the axilla. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of breast development, what characterizes Stage 2?","choices":["Preadolescent stage — elevation of only the nipple itself, with no breast bud formation present","Projection of the nipple only, with the areolae having receded back to the general breast contour","Breast bud stage — elevation of breast and nipple as a round mound, with increased areola diameter","Areola and nipple project forward together to form a separate secondary mound above the breast"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stage 2 is the breast bud stage, with elevation of the breast/nipple as a round mound and increasing areola diameter. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"What breast changes occur during pregnancy?","choices":["No changes occur in the breast during pregnancy, since hormonal shifts do not affect glandular or vascular tissue","Enlargement from glandular hyperplasia and increased vascularity, increased nodularity, and nipples/areolae darken and become more prominent","Breast size decreases due to glandular atrophy, reduced vascularity, and thinning of the areolar skin, resembling aging changes","Only the nipple changes color and size; the areola and surrounding glandular tissue remain completely unaffected"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pregnancy causes breast enlargement (glandular hyperplasia, increased vascularity), increased nodularity, and darkening/enlargement of the nipples and areolae. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is colostrum?","choices":["A small sebaceous gland elevation found scattered across the areolar surface","Thick yellow discharge from the nipple near the end of pregnancy","Age-related glandular atrophy replacing breast tissue with adipose fat","A normal congenital variant of nipple inversion present since birth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Colostrum is a thick yellow nipple discharge that appears near the end of pregnancy. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"What equipment is needed for the breast exam?","choices":["A tuning fork and pen light","A stethoscope and otoscope","No equipment is needed","A speculum and lubricant"],"answer":2,"exp":"The breast exam is noted as requiring no special equipment. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why might breast sensitivity to touch be increased at certain times?","choices":["If the patient is premenstrual, breasts tend to be more sensitive to touch","Breast sensitivity to touch is entirely unrelated to the menstrual cycle or hormones","Breast sensitivity to touch never varies with timing or hormonal fluctuation","Breast sensitivity to touch only increases significantly after menopause begins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Premenstrual timing is specifically noted as a cause of increased breast sensitivity to touch. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What draping/exposure approach is used during the breast exam?","choices":["No draping is ever used at any point, from initial inspection through the final palpation step","The chest remains fully gowned throughout the entire exam, with the gown only briefly lifted for palpation","Only one breast is ever exposed at any point, with the other kept gowned for the entire visit","Full chest exposure for inspection; after inspection, one breast can be covered while the other is examined"],"answer":3,"exp":"Full exposure is needed for inspection, after which one breast can be draped while the other is examined. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the sitting position used for during the breast exam?","choices":["Inspection — it exaggerates dimpling or retractions","Palpation only","Only for the axillary lymph node exam, not general inspection","Bimanual pelvic-style palpation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sitting position is used for inspection because it exaggerates dimpling or retraction findings. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"For the supine palpation position, what preparation helps spread the breast evenly over the chest?","choices":["No specific positioning is needed for palpation, since breast tissue spreads evenly on its own","Having the patient lie flat on her back with both arms relaxed flat at her sides throughout the exam","Having the patient remain sitting upright with her arms crossed throughout the palpation","Placing a pillow under the shoulder of the examining side and having the patient rest her arm under/over her head"],"answer":3,"exp":"A pillow under the ipsilateral shoulder plus the arm positioned over/under the head spreads the breast evenly, easing nodule detection. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"What color change on inspection may suggest cancer or an inflammatory condition?","choices":["Redness","Yellow discoloration","Pallor","Cyanosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Red coloration on breast inspection may suggest cancer or an inflammatory condition. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What thickening finding on breast inspection is concerning?","choices":["Thickening is only relevant during pregnancy","Thickening suggestive of breast cancer","Thickening only matters if bilateral","Thickening is always a normal, benign finding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thickening on inspection is a concerning finding suggestive of breast cancer. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What nipple finding is described as a possible normal variant, versus one that is concerning for cancer?","choices":["Inversion can be a normal variant; retraction is concerning for cancer","Both inversion and retraction always indicate an underlying malignancy requiring biopsy","Retraction is always a normal variant; inversion is always concerning for cancer","Both inversion and retraction are always normal, benign findings regardless of onset"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nipple inversion can be a normal variant, while retraction (a change to a previously normal nipple) is concerning for cancer. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"What nipple/skin condition is associated with rashes or discharge on breast inspection?","choices":["Nipple eczema","Paget's disease","Areolar rosacea","Nipple psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Paget's disease is the nipple/skin condition associated with rash and discharge findings on breast inspection. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"During dynamic inspection, what should the examiner observe for while the patient raises her arms and presses her hands against her hips?","choices":["Skin tethering, retraction, dimpling, and asymmetry of movement","Only the presence or character of nipple discharge","Only the size and firmness of the axillary lymph nodes","Only the skin temperature over the breast surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific findings to observe for during dynamic inspection maneuvers. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which finger pads are used for breast palpation, and what pattern is employed?","choices":["Two hands pressing simultaneously from opposite sides in a symmetric back-and-forth pattern","The whole palm of the hand, moving in a loose, unsystematic circular pattern","Finger pads of the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th fingers, using a systematic vertical-strip pattern","The thumb alone, tracing small circles around the areola and nipple"],"answer":2,"exp":"Breast palpation uses the finger pads of the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th fingers in a systematic vertical-strip pattern. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"What three pressure levels should be applied during systematic breast palpation?","choices":["Pressure level is not standardized","Light, medium, and deep pressure","Only light pressure, to avoid causing discomfort","Only deep pressure, to reach the chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systematic breast palpation applies light, medium, and deep pressure at each point. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does normal breast consistency depend on, and can it vary widely?","choices":["Consistency depends only on the patient's chronological age, not on tissue composition at all","Physiological nodularity is always abnormal and warrants immediate biopsy for evaluation","It varies widely and depends largely on the amount of adipose versus firmer glandular tissue","Consistency is always uniform and firm in every healthy patient, regardless of body composition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal breast consistency varies widely, largely depending on the relative amounts of adipose tissue versus firmer glandular tissue. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"What should the examiner feel for when assessing nodules on breast palpation?","choices":["Only masses that are tender or painful to direct palpation pressure","Nodules cannot be reliably distinguished from surrounding normal breast tissue at all","Any masses that are larger or qualitatively different than the rest of the breast tissue","Only masses located directly beneath the nipple and areolar complex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nodule assessment involves feeling for masses that stand out as larger or qualitatively different from surrounding tissue. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"For a patient with breast implants performing self-examination, what specific technique is recommended?","choices":["Press firmly inward at the edge of the implants to feel the ribs beneath, checking carefully for unusual lumps or bumps","Palpation should avoid the area directly over the implant entirely, examining only the surrounding tissue","Implants make self-examination unnecessary, since imaging alone detects any underlying abnormality","Only light, superficial pressure should ever be used near the implant edges, to avoid rupturing the shell"],"answer":0,"exp":"This technique helps detect lumps that might otherwise be obscured by the implant by pressing at the implant edges to feel the underlying ribs. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 36"}]};
